        },
        "elapsedMs": 14102,
        "text": "Since you enjoy **Adobe Premiere Pro** and have been learning its **Lumetri Color panel**, I’d start with these resources:\n\n### Free tutorials\n- **[Adobe Premiere tutorials](https://helpx.adobe.com/premiere-pro/tutorials.html)** — A reliable starting point for organizing footage, trimming clips, working with audio, adding titles, and exporting. Look for lessons with downloadable practice footage.\n- **Premiere Gal on YouTube** — Practical Premiere walkthroughs covering captions, effects, audio cleanup, and color correction.\n- **[Adobe Video & Motion / Frame.io Insider](https://blog.frame.io/)** — Detailed articles on editing workflows, codecs, color, and post-production. Especially useful as you move beyond the basics.\n\n### Structured courses\n- **[LinkedIn Learning](https://www.linkedin.com/learning/)** — Search for *Premiere Pro Essential Training*. Good if you prefer a clear sequence rather than scattered tutorials. Some public libraries provide free access.\n- **[Inside the Edit](https://www.insidetheedit.com/)** — Focuses on the creative decisions behind editing: storytelling, pacing, selecting shots, and building sequences—not just which buttons to press. It’s a paid option.\n\n### Storytelling and editing theory\n- **Every Frame a Painting on YouTube** — Short video essays about film technique, including rhythm and visual storytelling. The archive is valuable even though it isn’t a software course.\n- **_In the Blink of an Eye_ by Walter Murch** — An approachable book about why cuts work and how emotion shapes editing decisions.\n\n**A useful first project:** Make a 30–60-second video using your own footage. Start with clean cuts and a clear sequence, then balance dialogue and music, and finally use Lumetri to match exposure and white balance between shots. Finish one small project before tackling lots of effects."
      },
